we got a lot to cover this morning and  for an in-depth look we're joined by  Kathleen Kingsbury deputy editorial page  editor of the New York Times Kathleen  good morning hi good morning  let's start with fire and fury since it  seems to be what everybody is talking  about and the president was tweeting  rather late last night for him about  this about this book and particularly  about his former chief strategist Steve  Bannon who's at the heart of this  account of the White House how how  significant do you think is the fallout  from the breakup of this relationship I  think what the fallout is really still  to be determined  Bannon is clearly the one who is  suffering the most right now we've seen  people like the Mercer's his financial  backers already start to distance  themselves the future of his website  Breitbart really it's remains to be seen  but I think we're gonna see more in the  next weeks there the revelations in this  book if if they are accurately reported  are scathing as far as the president's  fitness for office michael wolf is on  national television saying president  Trump has lost it is there a  reconciliation that needs to happen in  the Republican Party as far as this is  concerned I think that yes absolutely  that said I think that this book while  it lays out incredible details  particularly details directly from the  White House and quotes staffers in ways  that we haven't heard before  it really does echos a lot of the  reporting that we've heard over the last  year you know the president has been  erratic since he came into office you  know a lot of the concerns right now are  how do we minimize the damage  particularly on the world stage well so  how big a threat do you think it poses  on the world stage um you know we're  talking about nuclear war with North  Korea right now do we really want the  North Koreans to think that this is a  president who is impatient and unwilling  to have dialogue no absolutely not  you know we're seeing South Korea make  efforts at trying to reach out to the  north so the the u.s. really needs to  back that effort let's turn the page a  little bit to more White House intrigue  the New York Times your papers reporting  that President Trump refused  used to accept a potential recusal from  the Attorney General Jeff Sessions as  far as the Russia investigation what are  the implications here as far as  obstruction of justice and collusion  which seem to be the main prongs of  Robert Muller's investigation I think  the the biggest question from that  reporting this week Mike Michael Schmidt  is did the president try to obstruct  justice that's really the the main  question here it's a huge legal question  it's one that we've seen in the past  lead to impeachment hearings so I think  that that is one of the things that we  really need to look in the on the radar  in the next few weeks whether or not  that reporting is collaborated and  whether or not the DOJ starts digging  into it it sounds like there might be  more of a case to be made if you're  Muller on the obstruction of justice  exact rather than that exactly Russia I  think that right now the clearest  reporting that we have does show a White  House that's really been trying to stop  this investigation that has been trying  to do whatever it can to prevent  hearings in this matter at the same time  as we heard Jeff Pigasus report the  Justice Department is actively  investigating the Clinton Foundation  whether donors got preferential  treatment while Hillary Clinton was  Secretary of State this isn't the first  time the Foundation's been investigated  so so what's going on here you know I  think that given the fact that there  doesn't seem to be new evidence on the  table the only conclusion we can come to  is that this is a political vendetta  brought on by the White House you know I  think that the president has made it  clear that he wants the Clintons to be  investigated over and over again over  the past year but you know the White  House me the FBI looked into these  allegations in 2016 and said there  wasn't enough evidence to actually bring  charges so what's new now both speaking  of political football back and forth we  know the president and some of his top  Republican leadership are meeting at  Camp David to discuss the 2018  legislative agenda immigration is a  football that has been tossed back and  forth here the White House seems  unrelenting in its desire to have a  border wall what is the future for young  dreamers and those affected by the the  ending of daca you know I don't I think  the president has suggested that he  would like to find a path for  for the dreamers but the border wall is  one of the things that he said has to be  part of that deal you know that's a  non-starter for Democrats and I think  that you know what we really need here  is a compromise and you know this is a  group of talented young people that  America really can't afford to lose so  it would really be wonderful to see the  White House find a solution that  everyone can live with we will see how  that all plays out Kathleen Kingsbury  with the New York Times thanks for your  time thank you
 

